      "details": "{\"id\": \"claim:pair-1ed7d480c307658eaecd\", \"subject\": \"object:mpo-1385\", \"predicate\": \"suggested_association\", \"object\": \"object:mpo-8994\", \"basis\": \"computational_suggestion\", \"status\": \"unresolved\", \"source_stance\": \"suggested\", \"polarity\": \"positive\", \"attributed_to\": \"agent:matcher\", \"recorded_at\": \"2026-11-02\", \"source_statement_date\": null, \"scope\": \"book relationship\", \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"source:340f945088b424d674d1\", \"locator\": \"pair-1ed7d480c307658eaecd\"}], \"supports\": [], \"challenges\": [], \"support_notes\": [\"Saved compatibility ranking only; no new direct pair-level source interpretation was performed by this triage.\", \"One broad notated field with service rubrics; catalogue Holy-Thursday context is credited. The two catalogue IX/D marks are clipped in retained first/third views; no secure fresh complete reading. / One broad notated field with already studied Stephen material. Previously recorded IIII-like and k-like red signs recur; no new side map or physical join.\"], \"challenge_notes\": [\"Shared style, liturgy, inherited metadata and capture appearance remain explanations; differing catalogue assignments are not automatic negatives.\", \"The published data assigns the objects to different reconstruction units; this is contextual counterevidence, not automatic proof of different books.\", \"One broad field and different service/calendar material are compatibility only. Original mark function, resets, section copying and composite volumes remain alternatives. Reused bindings do not establish original quire structure.\"], \"source_locator\": null, \"contextual_evidence\": [], \"lead_id\": \"pair-1ed7d480c307658eaecd\"}"
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:lost-library:claim%3Apair-1b35a032653fb22b9e36",
      "@type": "ll:Claim",
      "subject": {
        "@id": "urn:lost-library:object%3Ampo-20106"
      },
      "relationship": "ll:same_codex",
      "object": {
        "@id": "urn:lost-library:object%3Ampo-8201"
      },
      "status": "previously_documented",
      "basis": "researcher_interpretation",
      "polarity": "positive",
      "attributedTo": {
        "@id": "urn:lost-library:agent%3Aproject-ai"
      },
      "details": "{\"id\": \"claim:pair-1b35a032653fb22b9e36\", \"subject\": \"object:mpo-20106\", \"predicate\": \"same_codex\", \"object\": \"object:mpo-8201\", \"basis\": \"researcher_interpretation\", \"status\": \"previously_documented\", \"source_stance\": \"asserted\", \"polarity\": \"positive\", \"attributed_to\": \"agent:project-ai\", \"recorded_at\": \"2026-11-02\", \"source_statement_date\": null, \"scope\": \"book relationship\", \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"source:36fb90793e201dc844e5\", \"locator\": \"H07\"}], \"supports\": [], \"challenges\": [], \"support_notes\": [\"Grajewski2022 p293 reports a continuous antiphon across20106 and8201.\", \"The old A 103 reference was used for a separate National Library fragment collection deposited at the National Archives; a different A 103 k fragment is explicitly listed there. Together with the Fr 20106 source fields this makes the deposit a plausible collection-level lead, while the matching psalter label alone cannot identify Fr 20106.\", \"The higher-resolution Fr8201 photograph provisionally reads the surviving ending pulchrior sponso superno placuit, followed on the same visible surface by Traditur ergo a patre doctoribus. The beginning of the first chant is not visible here.\", \"On the retained Fr8201 native image, after the Specie ending and Traditur candidate, the next decorated opening is provisionally Nobilis et pulchra virgo katherina beata, with later spernit vaga gaudia mundi. The reading is unblinded and not independently verified.\", \"Genre, reported year and Sziget connection make Gábriel's no. 167 a plausible candidate for the manuscript behind Toldy's mediated wording. This is an exploratory identification hypothesis, not a demonstrated crosswalk.\", \"The institutional biographical context is consistent with, but does not prove, a connection to the Lyceum that historically deposited the 1399 no. 167 breviary. Academy catalogue and index favor 1850 as the cited volume date, leaving the reason for the 1861 almanac's 1851 listing unresolved.\", \"The retained Fr 8304 capture 4 appears to read sollempni[a?] followed by suscipiat alacriter pia ma[...], rather than the E49 sollempnitas. This is an AI-assisted, reference-exposed correction from a 1000-pixel image; the final letter and cropped continuation remain qualified.\", \"Grajewski 2025, p.78 note 11, reports two Catherine office variants in Pal. lat. 527 beginning with the same shortened Inclita antiphon. The current image recheck provides two visible Inclita openings after Catherine-related headings, but does not verify every word or full liturgical identity.\", \"The existing MPO export reports one leaf for Fr 20106 and lists Clement, Catherine, Barbara and Novo decurrens lumine lascivit sol in virgine. The raw BOMPAC workbook repeats the description and is not independent evidence.\"], \"challenge_notes\": [\"20106 has no verified local image, so the visual comparison remains unevaluable.\", \"None of these sources identifies the exact present-day holder or photo of Fr 20106, establishes a physical crosswalk to the psalter, or proves the 20106/8201 or 8201/8304 book relationships. The reference dates to historical descriptions and the reasoning about Fr 20106 is an inference.\", \"Cantus indexed navigation for Vienna Cod.1799 f223v lists Specie corporis (204720) immediately before Traditur ergo (204938). The complete indexed first chant includes the ending. No Vienna source image was inspected. The Catherine feast table lists 46 entries for 204720, not exact-text or unique-book counts.\", \"The two consecutive chant candidates are already documented together in a different indexed witness. This limits their value as a standalone book identifier; it neither disproves the published adjacent-fragment proposal nor establishes the likelihood of that leaf split. Fr20106 still needs direct examination.\", \"Veselovská’s transcription of MUS I 12 lists the same three candidate chant IDs in order across f1r/1v, but reports prudens ... puella and vana in the third chant. The ellipses at the side change leave intervening text uncertain; no MUS I 12 manuscript image was inspected.\", \"Toldy's 1855 edition reports the virgo ... beata and vaga wording in another breviary source, so that wording is not new. Indexed Karlsruhe Aug.LX reports virgo ... puella and vaga; its original was not inspected. Prior notice of this exact wording specifically in Fr8201 remains uncleared.\", \"MUS I 12's reported leaf-side change is after the whole Specie antiphon; the published Fr20106/8201 proposal instead splits that chant after sed fide. The comparison is not a counterexample matching that precise page boundary.\", \"No direct link between the descriptions or Catherine text in no. 167 is documented here. Private historic foliation, a changed shelfmark or different book remain possible; current custody and source independence are unknown. The earlier Szilágyi article is unread and cited as 1850 by Toldy versus 1851 by an indexed almanac.\", \"The original Szilágyi article and medieval manuscript remain unread. Neither the book crosswalk, direct author access, current holding, common legal identity of school names, nor historical source independence is established. No link to Fr 8201 or Fr 20106 follows.\", \"Grajewski 2025 p.78 note 11 reports that Vatican Pal. lat. 527, a compilatory breviary, contains two Catherine offices, Ave virgo speciosa and Inclita sanctae virginis, both opening with the same shortened Inclita antiphon.\", \"The attributed case challenges a general assumption that a second Catherine office or repeated opening chant cannot occur in one medieval book. It does not match the full 2022 Ant49 argument unless the same explicit service rubric and original target passages are verified. The article describes different office variants; it does not establish that both are the same office in every detail.\", \"Pal. lat. 527 was not examined directly and no duplicate rubric was verified. Fr 20106 remains uninspected. A compilatory breviary is not necessarily structurally equivalent to the target antiphonary. The example is published prior art, not a newly discovered counterexample or an exclusion of the published Fr 8304 finding. The provenance or extent of the manuscript's distinct hands and sections have not been resolved.\"], \"source_locator\": null, \"contextual_evidence\": [\"evidence:O-mpo-8201-3-11\"], \"lead_id\": \"pair-1b35a032653fb22b9e36\"}"
    },
    {
      "@id": "urn:lost-library:claim%3Apair-3dbddb1743a8f10fe6f2",
      "@type": "ll:Claim",
      "subject": {
        "@id": "urn:lost-library:object%3Ampo-20274"
      },
      "relationship": "ll:same_codex",
      "object": {
        "@id": "urn:lost-library:object%3Ampo-8994"
      },
      "status": "unresolved",
      "basis": "researcher_interpretation",
      "polarity": "positive",
      "attributedTo": {
        "@id": "urn:lost-library:agent%3Aproject-ai"
      },
      "details": "{\"id\": \"claim:pair-3dbddb1743a8f10fe6f2\", \"subject\": \"object:mpo-20274\", \"predicate\": \"same_codex\", \"object\": \"object:mpo-8994\", \"basis\": \"researcher_interpretation\", \"status\": \"unresolved\", \"source_stance\": \"suggested\", \"polarity\": \"positive\", \"attributed_to\": \"agent:project-ai\", \"recorded_at\": \"2026-11-02\", \"source_statement_date\": null, \"scope\": \"book relationship\", \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"source:36fb90793e201dc844e5\", \"locator\": \"H03\"}], \"supports\": [], \"challenges\": [], \"support_notes\": [\"Fr20274 has two overlapping source captures; Fr8994 has four partial captures. A simple one-image-per-side interpretation would be unsafe.\", \"Two visible antiphon incipits agree with the office sequence in Hallqvist2020 edited C435, printedp6/PDF53. The higher-resolution invitatory comparison now distinguishes edited C435, its apparatus-reported manuscript state and edited C23 (PDF67).\", \"An older Finnish IV157 description already places Swedish account48/ChristiernPhilipssons1610 between Helsinki ff7and8. The pinned CSV uniquely maps that description to Fr10370. This is contextual published evidence, not a new placement or proof for every Codex1505 leaf.\", \"Two actual C23 source pages were acquired from Uppsala Alvin. A declared ten-site comparison retains eight target-evaluable word/structure sites and two unavailable target sites; corrected text, cancelled text and editorial reports remain separate.\", \"Supporting Fr10370 context now has literal verse-prefix/suffix evidence for source-image2→3 order. This improves intrinsic orientation without proving a new relationship involving Fr8994.\", \"The raw MPO note for Fr 8994 describes it as similar to Ant 99 and Helsinki Ant 156/157, cites Taitto 2001, pp. 161–168, and also points to Fr 1385. This is a prior catalogue similarity claim; it does not state that Fr 8994 and Fr 20274 are the same codex.\", \"The updated official F.m.IV.173 record lists Ant 99 as a suggested relationship within a broader stylistic/liturgical group; it does not call Ant 99 a proven same-codex member. This is published prior art for a possible wider production relationship, not direct identity evidence.\", \"The official F.m.IV.157 entry places the St Olaf office at fol. 8 and records opening-signature notation '[k] iiijr - k [v]'; Fr 8994's catalogue foliation is '1r IIII; 1v k'. This is a possible correspondence to test against images, not an established link.\", \"E32 inspected the local first Fr 8994 capture: a source-linked red four-stroke upper-margin mark is consistent with the catalogue's IIII transcription. This checks one visible mark only; side orientation and the paired k are not independently established.\"], \"challenge_notes\": [\"Office identity and broad square notation are not unique. No verified physical adjacency or melody/variant alignment found.\", \"The short comparison established no distinctive variant: agreement with a published office sequence is shared liturgical evidence, not independent evidence for the proposed physical association.\", \"The apparent absence of separate pium and Matri…condolentes before procida… agree with the standard edited wording, not a unique book fingerprint. The edition reports different states for C435. No direct inspection of C435 manuscript images is claimed.\", \"No hymn entry is visible between Venite and Immolandus in the inspected continuous target region. This is a written-layout observation; abbreviated practice, different book function or a hymn elsewhere remain alternatives.\", \"No distinctive shared uncorrected C23 reading is established. Seven selected sites give current/standard word or cue agreements; the eighth has a visible C23 hymn entry where the target continuous region has none. These are not eight independent identity features or a transmission tree.\", \"The predeclared Fr10370 boundary ROIs did not pass translation or SIFT/RANSAC registration; the same feature screen also produces small low-inlier fits for the deliberately different 2/3 control. These non-results do not prove different faces but remove no physical ambiguity.\", \"E26's checksum-verified BOMPAC workbook assigns Fr 20274/Ant 99/IV.157 to BOMPAC-912 and Fr 8994/Codex 1505/IV.156 to BOMPAC-5324. This is a published separate reconstruction, but it is research metadata rather than a fresh direct image examination; it does not by itself rule out a more complex relationship.\", \"E27's checksum-verified BOMPAC query puts F.m.IV.173, Ant 242/Fr 29666 and Hy 49/Fr 25592 in BOMPAC-9531, while Ant 99/IV.157 remain in BOMPAC-912. The different reconstruction units clarify the published disagreement but are not fresh image proof.\", \"Walta 2013's indexed excerpt places IV.156, IV.157 and IV.173 in a larger stylistic/production group, but does not say these are one codex. Its categories therefore cannot be treated as a direct contradiction of the narrower BOMPAC reconstruction units.\", \"The official Fragmenta catalogue describes IV.156 and IV.157 as stylistically similar but does not say they are the same codex; Codex 1505 is listed as a suggestion, while other links receive explicit same-codex labels.\", \"The official Fragmenta entry calls Codex 1505 a suggested relation to IV.157, not a demonstrated same-codex member; the possible k/IV signature alignment has not been checked against F.m.IV.157 and Fr 20278 images.\", \"The official F.m.IV.156 catalogue says the opening-signature convention was not infrequent in late-medieval codices and early printed books. The format alone therefore has limited specificity and is not a unique Codex 1505 fingerprint.\", \"The apparent same-address lead is now a potential content conflict, not simple corroborating signature similarity. The local third-nocturn text differs from the catalogue's first-nocturn range at the proposed matching address. The exclusion remains conditional on unverified numbering/section/source premises.\"], \"source_locator\": null, \"contextual_evidence\": [\"evidence:O-mpo-20274\", \"evidence:O-mpo-8994-1-9\", \"evidence:O-mpo-20274-1-13\", \"evidence:O-mpo-20274-2-14\", \"evidence:O-mpo-20274-2-15\", \"evidence:O-mpo-20274-2-16\", \"evidence:T-20274-invitatory\", \"evidence:T-20274-order\", \"evidence:T-20274-transition\", \"evidence:T-20274-patri-parens\", \"evidence:T-20274-dampnatur\", \"evidence:T-20274-eructavit-rident\", \"evidence:I32-8994-red-four-stroke-mark\"], \"lead_id\": \"pair-3dbddb1743a8f10fe6f2\"}"
    },
