      "details": "{\"id\": \"claim:pair-bef99c0c6dd2ef1ff931\", \"subject\": \"object:mpo-8201\", \"predicate\": \"same_codex\", \"object\": \"object:mpo-8304\", \"basis\": \"source_assertion\", \"status\": \"previously_documented\", \"source_stance\": \"rejected\", \"polarity\": \"negative\", \"attributed_to\": \"agent:institutional-source\", \"recorded_at\": \"2026-11-02\", \"source_statement_date\": \"2022\", \"scope\": \"book relationship\", \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"source:3dea922ca47ba7b1cf0d\", \"locator\": \"H06\"}], \"supports\": [], \"challenges\": [], \"support_notes\": [\"Grajewski2022 p293 argues for20106–8201 textual continuity and rejects8304 through duplicated office-opening structure despite the shared hand. Source paragraph/table re-read and visually checked.\", \"Selected De-initial D examples share pale fields, red stems, lozenges and toothed contours despite the published different-book decision. This is descriptive corroboration of the limits of ornament, not an independently rediscovered rejection.\", \"Fr8304 visibly preserves the Catherine/service rubric and opening incipit. Fr8201 preserves later office material and the cited pulchrior... continuation, not a second complete office-opening rubric in these retained photos.\", \"The old A 103 reference was used for a separate National Library fragment collection deposited at the National Archives; a different A 103 k fragment is explicitly listed there. Together with the Fr 20106 source fields this makes the deposit a plausible collection-level lead, while the matching psalter label alone cannot identify Fr 20106.\", \"The higher-resolution Fr8201 photograph provisionally reads the surviving ending pulchrior sponso superno placuit, followed on the same visible surface by Traditur ergo a patre doctoribus. The beginning of the first chant is not visible here.\", \"On the retained Fr8201 native image, after the Specie ending and Traditur candidate, the next decorated opening is provisionally Nobilis et pulchra virgo katherina beata, with later spernit vaga gaudia mundi. The reading is unblinded and not independently verified.\", \"Genre, reported year and Sziget connection make Gábriel's no. 167 a plausible candidate for the manuscript behind Toldy's mediated wording. This is an exploratory identification hypothesis, not a demonstrated crosswalk.\", \"The institutional biographical context is consistent with, but does not prove, a connection to the Lyceum that historically deposited the 1399 no. 167 breviary. Academy catalogue and index favor 1850 as the cited volume date, leaving the reason for the 1861 almanac's 1851 listing unresolved.\", \"The retained Fr 8304 capture 4 appears to read sollempni[a?] followed by suscipiat alacriter pia ma[...], rather than the E49 sollempnitas. This is an AI-assisted, reference-exposed correction from a 1000-pixel image; the final letter and cropped continuation remain qualified.\", \"Grajewski 2025, p.78 note 11, reports two Catherine office variants in Pal. lat. 527 beginning with the same shortened Inclita antiphon. The current image recheck provides two visible Inclita openings after Catherine-related headings, but does not verify every word or full liturgical identity.\"], \"challenge_notes\": [\"Duplicate text is not a universal automatic exclusion: composite volumes or repeated offices need contextual assessment. Our reading of8201 rubric is incomplete.\", \"The planned two-local-opening comparison cannot be completed from8201/8304 alone. The published argument uses Fr20106 as well, so a full local replication requires that missing source and its correct catalogue/holding identification. The published rejection remains attributed and unchanged.\", \"None of these sources identifies the exact present-day holder or photo of Fr 20106, establishes a physical crosswalk to the psalter, or proves the 20106/8201 or 8201/8304 book relationships. The reference dates to historical descriptions and the reasoning about Fr 20106 is an inference.\", \"Cantus indexed navigation for Vienna Cod.1799 f223v lists Specie corporis (204720) immediately before Traditur ergo (204938). The complete indexed first chant includes the ending. No Vienna source image was inspected. The Catherine feast table lists 46 entries for 204720, not exact-text or unique-book counts.\", \"The two consecutive chant candidates are already documented together in a different indexed witness. This limits their value as a standalone book identifier; it neither disproves the published adjacent-fragment proposal nor establishes the likelihood of that leaf split. Fr20106 still needs direct examination.\", \"Veselovská’s transcription of MUS I 12 lists the same three candidate chant IDs in order across f1r/1v, but reports prudens ... puella and vana in the third chant. The ellipses at the side change leave intervening text uncertain; no MUS I 12 manuscript image was inspected.\", \"Toldy's 1855 edition reports the virgo ... beata and vaga wording in another breviary source, so that wording is not new. Indexed Karlsruhe Aug.LX reports virgo ... puella and vaga; its original was not inspected. Prior notice of this exact wording specifically in Fr8201 remains uncleared.\", \"MUS I 12's reported leaf-side change is after the whole Specie antiphon; the published Fr20106/8201 proposal instead splits that chant after sed fide. The comparison is not a counterexample matching that precise page boundary.\", \"No direct link between the descriptions or Catherine text in no. 167 is documented here. Private historic foliation, a changed shelfmark or different book remain possible; current custody and source independence are unknown. The earlier Szilágyi article is unread and cited as 1850 by Toldy versus 1851 by an indexed almanac.\", \"The original Szilágyi article and medieval manuscript remain unread. Neither the book crosswalk, direct author access, current holding, common legal identity of school names, nor historical source independence is established. No link to Fr 8201 or Fr 20106 follows.\", \"Grajewski 2025 p.78 note 11 reports that Vatican Pal. lat. 527, a compilatory breviary, contains two Catherine offices, Ave virgo speciosa and Inclita sanctae virginis, both opening with the same shortened Inclita antiphon.\", \"The attributed case challenges a general assumption that a second Catherine office or repeated opening chant cannot occur in one medieval book. It does not match the full 2022 Ant49 argument unless the same explicit service rubric and original target passages are verified. The article describes different office variants; it does not establish that both are the same office in every detail.\", \"Pal. lat. 527 was not examined directly and no duplicate rubric was verified. Fr 20106 remains uninspected. A compilatory breviary is not necessarily structurally equivalent to the target antiphonary. The example is published prior art, not a newly discovered counterexample or an exclusion of the published Fr 8304 finding. The provenance or extent of the manuscript's distinct hands and sections have not been resolved.\"], \"source_locator\": \"p.293; exact textual/liturgical rejection retained in frozen H06\", \"contextual_evidence\": [\"evidence:O-mpo-8201-3-11\", \"evidence:O-mpo-8304-4-12\", \"evidence:C20-D-8201\", \"evidence:C20-D-8304\"], \"lead_id\": \"pair-bef99c0c6dd2ef1ff931\"}"
    },
